  "summary": "Toledo City Council deadlocked on a resolution to terminate the city's Flock camera contracts, with votes failing to advance both a two-week delay and the termination itself. Council also gave a first reading to competing legislation from Councilman Martinez that would retain the cameras but restrict uses including data sharing related to immigration, reproductive health, and gender-affirming care, with monthly reviews to remove agencies participating in ICE 287(g) agreements.",
  "key_quotes": [
    {
      "quote": "Toledo City Council remains deadlocked over the future of the city's Flock surveillance cameras after a series of votes failed to advance a proposal to terminate the city's contracts with the company.",
      "paragraph_idx": 0
    },
    {
      "quote": "The council first considered delaying a decision for two weeks on the proposal to terminate the city's Flock contracts and deactivate the cameras, pending the results of an ongoing audit of the city's use of the technology.",
      "paragraph_idx": 2
    },
    {
      "quote": "allows for monthly reviews of Flock data sharing to identify and manually remove law enforcement agencies that have agreements with U.S. Immigration and Customs Enforcement under the federal 287(g) program.",
      "paragraph_idx": 10
    }
  ],
